"A refrigerator cannot make cold, because cold is not a thing. Cold just means less heat. So a refrigerator does only one job: it takes heat out of the box and puts it somewhere else. To do that, it uses a trick you already know. When you climb out of a swimming pool, the water on your skin dries and you feel chilly. A liquid that evaporates takes heat away with it.": "42d013fc0eeb",
"A towel soaks up water.": "6958bfb18435",
"Fish spoils quickly on a hot day.": "26b1e2cec8d9",
"Here is a puzzle. On a hot day, can you cool the kitchen by leaving the refrigerator door open? No! The heat taken from inside comes right back out of the coil into the same room, together with the heat from the electricity. The kitchen gets warmer. So close the door quickly, do not pack the refrigerator too full, and leave some space around it so the heat can get away.": "80e70174a3a8",
"Inside the walls of a refrigerator there is a long tube with a special liquid in it, called refrigerant. In the part of the tube that is inside the box, the refrigerant is very cold, colder than the food. It evaporates and turns into a gas. As it does, it soaks up heat from the air and the food, just like the water drying on your skin.": "9c27a0dbb1e3",
"Next the gas goes to the compressor, the part that hums at the bottom of the refrigerator. The compressor squeezes the gas hard, and squeezing makes it hot, hotter than your kitchen. The hot gas flows through a coil on the outside. There it gives its heat to the room and turns back into a liquid. Then it is pushed through a very thin tube. Its pressure drops suddenly, it becomes very cold, and it goes back inside to start again.": "2db2ccc44dbd",
"Open the refrigerator on a hot day and cool air touches your face. Now put your hand on the side of the refrigerator, or near the back. On many refrigerators it feels warm! Why would a machine that keeps food cold be warm on the outside? The answer tells you how a refrigerator really works.": "b71418a176bc",
"Open the refrigerator on a hot day and cool air touches your face. Now put your hand on the side of the refrigerator, or near the back. On many refrigerators it feels warm! Why would a machine that keeps food cold be warm on the outside? The answer tells you how a refrigerator really works. A refrigerator cannot make cold, because cold is not a thing. Cold just means less heat. So a refrigerator does only one job: it takes heat out of the box and puts it somewhere else. To do that, it uses a trick you already know. When you climb out of a swimming pool, the water on your skin dries and you feel chilly. A liquid that evaporates takes heat away with it. Inside the walls of a refrigerator there is a long tube with a special liquid in it, called refrigerant. In the part of the tube that is inside the box, the refrigerant is very cold, colder than the food. It evaporates and turns into a gas. As it does, it soaks up heat from the air and the food, just like the water drying on your skin. Next the gas goes to the compressor, the part that hums at the bottom of the refrigerator. The compressor squeezes the gas hard, and squeezing makes it hot, hotter than your kitchen. The hot gas flows through a coil on the outside. There it gives its heat to the room and turns back into a liquid. Then it is pushed through a very thin tube. Its pressure drops suddenly, it becomes very cold, and it goes back inside to start again. People have used this idea for a long time. In 1834 Jacob Perkins built the first working machine that cooled things this way. Before home refrigerators, families kept food cold with big blocks of ice. The first electric refrigerators for homes were made in 1913. Today the refrigerant in most new refrigerators is a gas called isobutane. Here is a puzzle. On a hot day, can you cool the kitchen by leaving the refrigerator door open? No! The heat taken from inside comes right back out of the coil into the same room, together with the heat from the electricity. The 